## Do Home Remedies for Toenail Fungus Actually Work?

Some do. Not all of them, not for everyone, and not as quickly as most people expect  but there is genuine clinical signal behind a small number of natural treatments.

Onychomycosis is a fungal infection of the nail plate and nail bed, most commonly caused by dermatophytes, a group of fungi that consume keratin. The nail plate acts as a physical barrier, which is both what makes the infection persistent and what makes topical treatments difficult to evaluate. A remedy that shows strong antifungal activity in a petri dish does not automatically translate to results in a living nail.

The home remedies that show meaningful clinical evidence are tea tree oil and snakeroot extract. A handful of others, including the viral Listerine and vinegar soak and Vicks VapoRub, have plausible antifungal ingredients and substantial anecdotal support without the clinical trials to match. The rest, garlic, baking soda, hydrogen peroxide, pure bleach soaks, are either too weak to reach an established infection or actively risky.

One reality that applies to every option on this list: toenails grow approximately 1.5mm per month. A complete toenail replacement takes 9 to 12 months. A remedy working correctly shows as new, clear nail growing in at the base, not as the existing infected nail recovering. If you quit a working treatment at week three because the nail still looks the same, you are quitting too early. That is the most common reason people report that nothing has worked.

People also ask about wearing polish during treatment. If that is a concern alongside the infection itself, our post on <a href='/blog/toenail-fungus/nail-polish-toenail-fungus'>nail polish toenail fungus</a> covers what is and is not safe to do while treating an active infection.

