## The Viral Listerine, Vinegar, and Vicks Soak: Does It Hold Up?

The Listerine and vinegar combination has been circulating in online communities since at least the early 2000s, long before TikTok made it a trend. The typical protocol involves soaking the affected foot in equal parts amber Listerine and white vinegar for 20 to 30 minutes daily, with Vicks VapoRub applied to the nail overnight under a sock. People who report success describe doing this consistently for four to twelve months before seeing clear nail growth from the base.

To be direct: the standard Listerine and vinegar soak has antifungal ingredients but no clinical trial evidence specifically for toenail fungus. Amber Listerine contains thymol, eucalyptol, menthol, and methyl salicylate, all of which have documented antifungal activity in laboratory settings. White vinegar creates an acidic environment that inhibits fungal growth. Both of these biological mechanisms are real. The problem is penetration.

The nail plate is dense, hydrophobic keratin. Aqueous soaks, regardless of their ingredient profile, do not penetrate deeply enough to reliably reach the nail bed where an established dermatophyte infection lives. This is why podiatrists do not recommend soak-based remedies as primary treatments, even when the ingredients themselves are antifungal. Surface activity does not equal nail bed activity.

What the combination may do: inhibit spread to surrounding skin and other nails, reduce superficial fungal load on the nail surface, and provide a low-cost, consistent daily ritual that keeps the nail environment less hospitable to further growth. For mild infections that have not deeply penetrated the nail, the soak may be enough. For moderate to established infections, it is unlikely to clear the nail fully.

If you want to try it: use original amber Listerine, not flavoured varieties. Equal parts Listerine and white vinegar, 20 to 30 minutes per soak, daily. Trim and gently file the nail before soaking to reduce nail thickness and slightly improve penetration. Apply Vicks directly onto the nail and under the free edge at night, cover with a sock, repeat the following evening. Expect a minimum of three months before any visible change at the nail base.

