## Home Remedies to Avoid: Bleach, Urine, and Other Risky Trends

Bleach is not a safe home remedy for toenail fungus. This needs to be stated plainly because it still appears in listicles, in forum posts, and on some health sites with dosing instructions.

Bleach (sodium hypochlorite) kills fungi in laboratory conditions and on hard surfaces. This is the biological fact that drives the recommendation. But killing fungus in a petri dish and curing an infection living beneath a toenail are completely different problems. Toenail fungus lives under and within the nail plate, not on its surface. The nail plate is dense, hydrophobic keratin that acts as a physical shield. Bleach does not penetrate it. A foot soak in diluted bleach addresses surface organisms while the infection underneath continues undisturbed.

What the bleach does reach is the skin surrounding the nail, which is thin, delicate, and vulnerable. Even diluted bleach causes irritation, dryness, and cracking with repeated use. Cracked skin around the nail creates new entry points for infection. Concentrated bleach causes chemical burns. According to research in the Asian Journal of Research in Dermatological Science, topical bleach can actually increase the risk of fungal infection by damaging the skin barrier. Using bleach on toenail fungus risks making the situation worse, not better, and there is no clinical evidence it resolves onychomycosis.

For a thorough explanation of why <a href="https://www.healthline.com/health/dont-use-bleach-to-get-rid-of-toenail-fungus">bleach is not an effective toenail fungus treatment</a>, Healthline's coverage of the dermatological evidence is worth reading directly.

Urine therapy for toenail fungus has no credible scientific basis. The claim rests on urea content: urea does have softening properties on thickened nails, and pharmaceutical urea cream is legitimately used to debride nail tissue before treatment. Fresh urine contains only trace urea concentrations, far below therapeutic levels. It carries microbial contamination risks and should not be applied to broken skin or open nail margins.

Hydrogen peroxide applied directly to the nail surface has some surface-level antiseptic activity but does not penetrate the nail plate to reach a fungal infection. It is not considered an effective treatment for onychomycosis by dermatological guidelines.

